[META]
framework: claude-framework
file_id: workflow.FEATURE
class: universal
version: 1.0.0

[FEATURE]
overview: Rules specific to implementing a new feature
applies_when: User asks to "add / build / implement / create" something that doesn't exist yet
follows: PHASES.toon (all six phases apply; this file adds feature-specific constraints)

[RULE_1_FIND_EXISTING_PATTERN_FIRST]
what: Before designing anything, search the project for existing features that solve a similar problem
why: Most projects already have idiomatic patterns. Reinventing breaks consistency and creates duplication.
how:
  - In DISCOVER phase, grep/explore for similar features (e.g. for "add a new API endpoint" → find how other endpoints are structured)
  - Copy the structural pattern; only deviate when you can articulate why
  - Cite the reference in your plan: "Following the pattern used in <file>"

[RULE_2_MVP_FIRST]
what: The first version implements the minimum that satisfies the stated need
why: Scope creep is the #1 cause of plans that fail confirmation or produce buggy code
how:
  - In PLAN phase, explicitly list "Out of scope" items — things the user DIDN'T ask for
  - Resist symmetry: if user asks for "read", don't design "read + write" unless they asked
  - Resist configurability: hardcode values that have only one real use case today; don't add flags for hypothetical needs

[RULE_3_NO_PREMATURE_ABSTRACTION]
what: Three similar lines of code beats one clever abstraction
why: Abstraction locks in a shape before you know the right shape. Duplication is cheap; wrong abstraction is expensive.
how:
  - Don't introduce a helper / base class / factory / strategy on the FIRST occurrence of a pattern
  - Wait until you have ≥3 real instances before extracting
  - If the plan includes "new utility module / helper / abstraction" — justify with 3 concrete call sites

[RULE_4_PUBLIC_API_REQUIRES_EXTRA_CONFIRMATION]
what: Any change that introduces or modifies a public API (exported function, HTTP route, schema, CLI flag) is a GATE (see GATES.toon)
why: Public APIs are contracts. Getting them wrong forces painful migrations later.
how:
  - In PLAN phase, call out the new API surface explicitly
  - In CONFIRM phase, present API shape to user as a separate question: "Is this API signature correct?"
  - Do not implement until the specific API shape is approved (not just "the overall plan")

[RULE_5_TESTS_MATCH_PROJECT_STYLE]
what: Follow the project's existing test conventions — don't introduce a new framework or style
why: Mixed test styles are a maintenance tax; reviewer cognitive load goes up
how:
  - In DISCOVER phase, find existing tests for similar code; note the framework and style
  - If the project has no tests for similar code — ask the user: "Add tests? Which style?" Don't assume.
  - Integration tests go where integration tests live; unit tests where unit tests live

[RULE_6_FEATURE_FLAGS_ONLY_WHEN_ASKED]
what: Don't add a feature flag, config toggle, or "backwards compatibility shim" unless the user asks for it
why: These are liabilities (extra code paths, extra testing); they belong in the plan only if the user wants them
how:
  - If you think one might be needed → raise it as a question during CONFIRM, don't add it silently
  - "Just change the code" is the default

[RULE_7_NO_FUTURE_PROOFING]
what: Don't design for requirements the user hasn't stated
why: YAGNI. Future requirements usually look different than you guess.
how:
  - If you find yourself writing "in case we later want to..." → delete that code
  - Parameters / hooks / extension points with no current consumer are dead weight
  - Plugin systems, event buses, strategy patterns require ≥2 real consumers to justify

[FEATURE_PLAN_CHECKLIST]
Before leaving PLAN phase, confirm:
  - [ ] Referenced an existing similar pattern (or verified none exists)
  - [ ] MVP scope listed; "Out of scope" section is non-empty
  - [ ] No speculative abstractions
  - [ ] Public API surface called out explicitly (or confirmed: no new public API)
  - [ ] Test approach matches project conventions
  - [ ] No feature flags / toggles / shims unless user requested
